\ottava sets one or two octaves above or below the
written note(s) that follow it.
Since it concerns notes, it must be included in the
notes definition of an instrument, inside a bar.
The \ottava command can also be called with its shorter
alias, \ott.
Its syntax is the following:

\ottava 1

Its argument is either 1, 2, -1, -2, or 0.

It is used so the user can write a high or low note
but make is fit inside the staff. If, for example,
you want to write a C one octave below middle C, in a
staff with a G clef, then you can write the following:

\bar ott-1 {
    \time 4/4
    \inst c'2 \ottava -1 c
}

This will result in two identical notes in the score,
but the second will have the octave symbol written
underneath it. The sequencer will play the correct
pitces.

Note that octaves set with \ottava are persistent
until a new octave is set. If you want to remove the
octave symbol from your score, call it with a 0
argument. The example below sets two octaves above
at the beginning of the bar, and then removes the
octave symbol.

\bar ott-2 {
    \time 4/4
    \inst \ottava 2 c'''''2 \ottava 0 c''
}
